<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Dian Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">279 BCE – 109 BCE (approx.) · capital Jinning</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A bronze-working kingdom around Lake Dian in Yunnan, whose tombs at Shizhaishan show lively scenes of ritual, war and daily life cast on cowrie containers.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Dian grew among the farming and fishing peoples around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-dian">Lake Dian</a>, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kunming">Kunming</a>. Tradition holds that the Chu general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhuang-qiao">Zhuang Qiao</a> reached the lake around 279 BCE and, cut off by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> conquest of Chu's western lands, made himself king of Dian, but the kingdom's culture was overwhelmingly local.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Dian's elite were buried at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shizhaishan">Shizhaishan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lijiashan">Lijiashan</a> with bronze drums, weapons and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cowrie-shell">cowrie</a>-shell containers topped with miniature figures showing sacrifices, weaving, markets and battles. These reveal a stratified society of farmers, herders and craftsmen, with women prominent in ritual. Dian controlled routes south towards South-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a> and west to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/myanmar">Burma</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 109 BCE the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/han-dynasty">Han Dynasty</a> sent an army, and the king of Dian surrendered. Han created <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yizhou-commandery">Yizhou Commandery</a> but let the king keep his title, granting him a gold seal that was found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shizhaishan">Shizhaishan</a> in 1956. Dian culture faded into Han provincial life during the 1st century CE.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/dian-kingdom.jpg" alt="Dian Kingdom" width="320" height="198"><figcaption>Image: Qiushufang · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yue_tribes.png" target="